samples.evidence:
`offline-runtime.tsx:144-148` sets `reconciled = true` unconditionally once `purgeRevokedOfflineDocuments(...)` resolves, and the long comment at lines 155-173 justifies the gate: "Offering anyway when it failed is how content the user may no longer read gets copied back into their own workspace with their name on it."

But `purgeRevokedOfflineDocuments` never rejects on its own failure. `packages/frontend/src/lib/offline-erase.ts:461-488`:

```js
try {
  const revoked = (await store.storedDocumentIds({ archives: true })).filter(...);
  for (const id of revoked) { purged += await store.purgeDocument(id, {...}); }
  return purged;
} catch (err) {
  console.warn("[offline] could not reconcile local copies:", err);
  return 0;          // <-- swallowed
} finally { store.close(); }
```

It also returns `0` without throwing when `offlineUserId()` is falsy (line 452-456). So the only failure that reaches the caller's `catch` is `fetchDocuments()` rejecting — the *listing*. The *purge*, which is the half that actually deletes archives for revoked workspaces, can fail silently and the gate still reports success.

Vector: user is removed from workspace W; their device holds an archive (a full compressed snapshot) of W's document D. Next session `fetchDocuments()` succeeds, so the listing is fine. Inside the purge, `store.open()` rejects — reachable via `onblocked` (another tab holding a version change, see `wafflebase-doc-store.ts` `open()`'s `request.onblocked` reject) or any transaction abort — so `storedDocumentIds`/`purgeDocument` throws, the catch swallows, `reconciled` becomes `true`. `offerRecoverableWork` then runs, lists D's surviving archive, and offers "Save a copy" for content the user may no longer read.

The suite only covers the listing half (`offline-runtime.test.tsx`, "offers nothing back when the reconcile could not run" mocks `fetchDocuments.mockRejectedValue`), so the broken half is untested and the gate reads as enforced.

Fix: have `purgeRevokedOfflineDocuments` rethrow (or return a success flag the caller checks) rather than returning `0` on both the no-identity and the catch paths.

`offline-runtime.tsx:245-251` wraps `fetchDocument(described.id)` in a bare `catch { /* Keep the fallbacks. */ }`, leaving `workspaceId` undefined for *any* failure. `offline-copy-recovery.ts:174-184` then does:

```js
async function destinationWorkspace(given?: string): Promise<string> {
  if (given) return given;
  const workspaces = await fetchWorkspaces();
  const fallback = workspaces[0]?.id;   // the user's own workspace
  ...
}
```

The two cases being conflated are semantically opposite. A 404 means "deleted upstream, the archived work is yours" — the design's intended fallback. A 403 means "you were removed from this workspace and may no longer read this" — and the fallback then copies the archived full snapshot of that workspace's document into the first workspace `fetchWorkspaces()` lists for the user, as a new server-side document they own.

`assertOk` throws an `HttpError` carrying `.status` (`packages/frontend/src/api/http-error.ts:104-125`), so the distinction is available and simply is not made.

Reachable two ways: (a) the reconcile gate bypass reported separately, and (b) revocation that happens *after* the once-per-session reconcile ran — the recovery toasts are `duration: Infinity` (line 255), so the "Save a copy" action can be clicked hours or days later against a `workspaceId` captured at session start.

Fix: inspect `err instanceof HttpError && err.status === 403` and refuse recovery (leave the archive, or drop it) rather than falling back to the user's own workspace.

